<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Partial Info.plist for the SkiVideoViewer-iPad target, merged into the
	one Xcode generates from the INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings
	(GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays YES).

	UIFileSharingEnabled lives here because there is no
	INFOPLIST_KEY_UIFileSharingEnabled: Xcode only recognises the
	INFOPLIST_KEY_* names listed in CoreBuildSystem.xcspec, and that one is
	not among them, so setting it in project.yml reached the build settings
	but never reached the built plist. Its sibling
	INFOPLIST_KEY_LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace IS in the xcspec and so
	stays in project.yml.

	Together the two keys put the app's Documents folder under "On My iPad"
	in Files, which is how athletes.txt is meant to get onto the device.

	Keep this file minimal. Anything expressible as a recognised
	INFOPLIST_KEY_* belongs in project.yml instead; a key repeated here
	would shadow the generated value.

	CFBundleURLTypes is here for the same reason: it is an array of
	dictionaries, and project.yml's settings are flat build-setting values,
	so it has no INFOPLIST_KEY_* form to redirect through. This registers
	the custom scheme IOSOAuthPresenter.callbackScheme asks Google to
	redirect to (Sources/SkiVideoViewer/iOS/IOSOAuthPresenter.swift) —
	loopback redirects are blocked for iOS OAuth clients, so this scheme is
	how Google's authorization response reaches the app at all.
	IOSRedirectTests pins the two against each other.

	Two schemes, because there are two kinds of client. The reverse-DNS one
	belongs to the built-in client this app was registered with. The second,
	dk.dyregod.video-viewer, is the one every *club-supplied* client redirects
	through: a Google iOS client accepts <registered-bundle-id>:/oauth2redirect
	as well as the reverse-DNS form (measured 2026-08-15), so every club can
	register its own client against that one fixed bundle id and the app needs
	no scheme it could not know at build time. ClubOAuthClientTests pins it.

	The built-in client keeps its own scheme rather than moving to the shared
	one, because that is what it was registered with — re-registering it would
	break the home club's sign-in.
	-->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.googleusercontent.apps.1075094033080-autj2djudlifsgtr0rd51910531ig1qt</string>
				<string>dk.dyregod.video-viewer</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
